The values, outlets, targets and actions of the four controllers, then the
backend request, the server half, the response policy, the Angular
counterpart and the migration table.

## Attributes

The suite is built on
[Pragmatic drag and drop](https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/);
links below point at its documentation where a concept is theirs.

Four kinds of attribute appear on the markup, each with the standard Stimulus
spelling:

| Kind | Attribute | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Value | `data-<controller>-<name>-value`, camel case hyphenated | `data-sortable-lists--list-accepted-type-value="task"` |
| Outlet | `data-sortable-lists-<child controller>-outlet` | `data-sortable-lists-sortable-lists--list-outlet="#tasks [data-controller~='sortable-lists--list']"` |
| Target | `data-<controller>-target="<name>"` | `data-sortable-lists--item-target="handle"` |
| Action | `data-action="<event>-><controller>#<method>"`, params as `data-<controller>-<param>-param` | `data-action="click->sortable-lists--item#move"` |

When you pass them through a Rails `data:` hash, as the previews do, write the
controller with a double underscore and the rest with single ones:
`sortable_lists__list_accepted_type_value:`.

### `sortable-lists` (root)

#### Values

| Value | Type | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `moveUrlTemplate` | string | | URI template expanded with the dragged row's id; must expand to a relative same-origin URL |
| `moveUrlTemplates` | JSON object | | per-item-type templates keyed by the dragged row's type; `moveUrlTemplate` is the fallback |
| `optimistic` | boolean | `false` | appends `optimistic=true` to the move URL and keeps the client's placement when the server answers with an event alone |
| `selectionEnabled` | boolean | `false` | enables batch selection and its pointer and keyboard gestures |
| `collectionMoveUrl` | string | | same-origin collection endpoint receiving ordered `ids[]` for one or many items |
| `announcementScope` | string | `js.sortable_lists.selection` | translation scope for selection feedback |
| `moveAnnouncementScope` | string | `js.sortable_lists.announcements` | translation scope for move feedback |
| `selectionDescriptionId` | string | empty | shared description referenced by selected items' focus hosts through `aria-describedby` |
| `maxBatchSize` | number | `0` | maximum items per move; zero means no client limit; selection itself is not capped |

#### Outlets

| Outlet | Required | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `sortable-lists--list` | yes | every list under this root |
| `sortable-lists--item` | yes | every row under this root |
| `sortable-lists--scrollable` | when declared | every auto-scrolling container under this root |

### `sortable-lists--list`

#### Values

| Value | Type | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `type` | string | | the list's own type, sent as `list_type`; a list without one accepts no drops |
| `acceptedType` | string | | the item type this list accepts; omit to make the list display-only ([typing data](https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/core-package/recipes/typing-data)) |
| `id` | string | | sent as `list_id`; required as soon as two lists share a type |
| `name` | string | | announced to screen readers on a cross-list move |
| `dropPosition` | `start` or `end` | `end` | where a drop on the list's empty space lands |

### `sortable-lists--item`

#### Values

| Value | Type | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `id` | string | | the record id; expands `{id}` in the move URL and is sent as a neighbor's `prev_id` |
| `type` | string | | matched against lists' `acceptedType`; keys `moveUrlTemplates` ([typing data](https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/core-package/recipes/typing-data)) |
| `label` | string | | accessible name used in announcements and in the external drag data |
| `mobility` | `free`, `confined`, `fixed` | `free` | controls selection and movement; unknown values are treated as `fixed`; see Sibling lists |
| `menuLabelKey` | string | | translation key pluralized by action scope size; omit to preserve the server-rendered menu name |
| `hideUnavailable` | boolean | `true` | hide inapplicable move actions; `false` disables them instead |
| `externalUrl` | URL | | when set, a drag also carries `text/uri-list`, `text/plain` and, with a label, `text/html` for targets outside the page ([data for external consumers](https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/core-package/adapters/element/about#data-for-external-consumers-getinitialdataforexternal), [external adapter](https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/core-package/adapters/external/about)) |

#### Targets

| Target | Cardinality | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `handle` | one | the element that starts a drag; the whole row when absent ([drag handles](https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/core-package/adapters/element/about#drag-handles)) |
| `preview` | one | the element that follows the pointer; the row when absent ([drag previews](https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/core-package/adapters/element/drag-previews)) |
| `moveItem` | one per move action | a move action; carries the `direction` param |
| `moveMenu` | one | the submenu or group wrapping the move actions, hidden or disabled when none applies |
| `focus` | one | focus host inside the row; the item itself when absent |
| `destinationItem` | one per destination action | carries JSON destination candidates in `data-sortable-lists-destinations` |
| `invokerGroup` | one | singular actions for the invoking item |
| `batchGroup` | one | actions for the resolved scope, hidden when none is presented |
| `groupDivider` | one | separates invoker and batch groups; visible only when both groups are presented |

#### Actions

| Action | Params | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `move` | `direction`: `top`, `up`, `down` or `bottom` | reorders the resolved scope without a drag; a batch must be contiguous in one list |
| `moveToDestination` | | submits the resolved scope to the destination item's single candidate |
| `prepareDialog` | event detail: `form` | supplies ordered `ids[]` to the destination form; cancels if the scope is refused or the root is busy |
| `focusItem` | | focuses the `focus` target, falling back to the item |

### `sortable-lists--scrollable`

#### Values

| Value | Type | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `allowedAxis` | `vertical`, `horizontal` or `all` | `vertical` | which edges trigger auto-scroll |
| `maxScrollSpeed` | `standard` or `fast` | `standard` | |

## The move request

`PUT` to the expanded move URL with `responseKind: 'turbo-stream'` and three
form fields:

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `list_type` | the destination list's `type` |
| `list_id` | the destination list's `id`; blank when it declares none, required as soon as two lists share a type |
| `prev_id` | id of the row the moved row now follows; blank when it landed first |

`prev_id` is always a record id, whatever the server orders by underneath. A
drop that leaves the row where it started sends no request.

### Collection moves

A selection-enabled consumer supplies `collectionMoveUrl` instead of expanding
one URL per item. The request adds ordered `ids[]` to the destination fields
above, for a single item as well as a batch. In Backlogs, an omitted `prev_id`
appends after the last non-batch item; an explicitly blank value inserts at the
top. A nonblank value identifies the anchor before the block.

Backlogs authorizes every member and applies the batch atomically, with a
server limit of 500 items. The root's `maxBatchSize` provides early feedback
but does not replace server validation. This collection contract is separate
from the single-record ordering implementations below.

## The server half

Two ordering families back this request. A blank `prev_id` moves the
record to the top in both. What happens to an anchor that is unknown,
outside the destination's scope, or the moved record itself depends on the
implementation:

* `acts_as_list` models include `Lists::MoveAfterAnchor`, which resolves the
  anchor within a scope you pass and reinserts relative to it. It refuses
  such anchors, returning `false` without mutating anything.
* Backlogs' own `move_after` removes the row from its list before looking
  the anchor up, and an anchor it cannot find puts the row at the top
  instead of refusing. The streamed list reconciles the client afterwards;
  the Sibling lists tab describes when Backlogs reloads for that reason.
* Models ordering by an array of keys insert after the anchor's key,
  translating the incoming id to that key first.

A move that changes list is a reparent plus an insert: one transaction,
both lists locked, so a failed insert leaves the record in its original
list. Answer refusals with `422` after streaming your own message; the client
keeps that status silent apart from the rollback. Any other failure raises a
generic toast.

## Response policy

The row moves in the DOM before the request resolves.

* **Morph reconciliation (default).** The endpoint streams the re-rendered
  list; the morph replaces the client's placement with the server's order.
* **Event-only (`optimistic`).** The endpoint may acknowledge with an event
  alone. The server decides per request whether the client's placement is
  trustworthy; see the Sibling lists tab.

A failed request rolls the rows back, unless a concurrent morph has already
moved them.

## Angular counterpart

Angular surfaces use the `opSortableLists`, `opSortableListsList` and
`opSortableListsItem` directives in
`frontend/src/app/shared/directives/sortable-lists/`, backed by the engine
in `frontend/src/common/drag-and-drop/sortable-lists-engine.ts`. Both
suites build on Pragmatic drag and drop and follow the same styling
conventions (`data-dragging`, `data-drop-position`, `data-drop-container`),
but share no code.

They differ on persistence, and this paragraph is only an overview; the
event interfaces in `sortable-lists.directive.ts` are the contract. A drop
emits an `opSortableListsDrop` event carrying the target and edge. The
component persists the result itself, then calls the event's
`complete(success)`; until it does, the root stays busy and refuses further
drags, warning after ten seconds. A row leaving for another list also emits
`opSortableListsRemoved` on its source list, settled with `finalize()`.
There is no `prev_id` request. The draggable autocompleter uses these
directives; new server-rendered surfaces use the Stimulus suite.

## Used in

* Text transform actions (`Admin::TextTransformActions`): one list.
* Backlogs (`modules/backlogs`): sibling lists, scrollable columns,
  optimistic placement, confined rows, external drag data.
* Project Attributes (`Settings::ProjectCustomFieldSections`): nested lists
  with per-type move endpoints.

## Migrating off `generic-drag-and-drop`

| `generic-drag-and-drop` | `sortable-lists` |
| --- | --- |
| `generic_drag_and_drop_target: "container"` | a `sortable-lists--list` controller on the list |
| `generic_drag_and_drop_target: "scrollContainer"` | a `sortable-lists--scrollable` controller |
| `drop-url` on the row | `move-url-template` on the root |
| `draggable-type` / `target-allowed-drag-type` | the row's `type` and the list's `accepted-type` |
| index-based position params | `list_type` + `list_id` + `prev_id` |
| no keyboard path | `moveItem` menu actions |

The endpoint changes shape too: Dragula posted an index, so a server that
still computes positions from one needs porting to anchor semantics before
its list can move over.
